\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Coordinate tests for measurable maps}
\date{2026-10-25}

\p{Let #{V} be finite and #{(Y,\mathcal T)} a measurable space.
A map #{f:Y\to X_V} is measurable if and only if every map
#{y\mapsto f(y)(v)} to the Borel real line is measurable.
Continuous maps between finite real field spaces are Borel measurable.
In particular this holds for linear maps, coordinate restriction,
zero extension and polynomial functions. Compare
\citet{Exercise 1.4.29 and Remark 1.4.33, pp. 95-96, preliminary version}{tao2011measure}.}
\proof{
\p{Necessity follows by preimages of cylinders. For sufficiency, the
sets #{A\subseteq X_V} with #{f^{-1}(A)\in\mathcal T} form a
sigma-algebra containing the cylinders; use \ref{fdip-002E}.
For a continuous map, preimages of open sets are open. The same
sigma-algebra argument extends this to all Borel sets. Linearity gives
continuity by \ref{fdip-002C}; finite sums and products give the
polynomial case.}
\p{For #{R\subseteq V}, restriction #{\rho_R:X_V\to X_R} sends
#{x} to #{x|_R}. Zero extension #{\iota_R:X_R\to X_V} is #{y} on
#{R} and zero elsewhere. Both are linear and measurable, and
#{\rho_R\iota_R=\operatorname{id}_{X_R}}. The reverse composite
zeros the removed coordinates and need not be the identity. This is
precisely the real [polynomial pullback pair](fdip-002A), now with
its Borel structures; it is not a claim about preserving reference volume.}
}
\p{The product-measurability equivalence is
\leanref{measurable_pi_iff}; it concerns measurable products, without
asserting topology or integrability. For Borel spaces an inducing map
also detects measurability through composition, as in
\leanref/tauceti{Topology.IsInducing.measurable_comp_iff}; no measurable
range is required. Neither statement attaches a state to every observable.}
